## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of temporal, multi-step constraints in a 3-valued logic setting. Requires constructing a concrete model and outlining a backtracking propagator that preserves non-F constraints while allowing Unknowns.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene three-valued semantics for implication
- Temporal constraint propagation across time steps
- Unit/branching propagation in backtracking

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: evaluateImp(A,B) returns Kleene(A,B)
function evaluateImp(A,B){ // A,B in {T,F,U}
  // ¬A ∨ B with Kleene ordering T>U>F
  const not = {T:'F', F:'T', U:'U'}[A];
  const order = { 'F':0, 'U':1, 'T':2 };
  // ⩒ is max by value order
  const values = [not, B];
  let best = 'F';
  for (const v of values) if (order[v] > order[best]) best = v;
  return best;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a tiny 3-valued solver: propagation, clash detection, and controlled backtracking while preserving non-boolean placeholders.

## Key Concepts
- 3-valued logic propagation
- Forward-checking and fixpoint computation
- Backtracking with Unknowns to preserve flexibility

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(vars, constraints) {
  // vars: {S1: T|F|U, S2: T|F|U, S3: T|F|U}
  // constraints: list of functions returning {changed, ok}
  // Minimal sketch for illustration
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const c of constraints) {
      const res = c(vars);
      if (res.changed) changed = true;
      if (!res.ok) return {ok: false};
    }
  }
  return {ok: true, vars};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a small 3-valued constraint solver and to enforce an exact-one-U constraint, a common pattern in policy engines and static analyzers.

## Key Concepts

- Kleene three-valued logic
- Constraint propagation
- Backtracking with pruning
- Exact-one constraint

## Code Example

```javascript
function satisfies(A,B,C){
  // A,B,C in {T,F,U}; returns true if all constraints not-F
  const not = v => v=== 'T' ? 'F' : v==='F' ? 'T' : 'U';
  const and = (x,y) => (x==='F'||y==='F')?'F':(x==='T'&&y==='T')?'T':'U';
  const or = (x,y) => (x==='T'||y==='T')?'T':(x==='F'&&y==='F')?'F':'U';
  const imp = (x,y) => not(x) || y;
  const c1 = imp(and(A,B), C);
  const c2 = imp(not(B), or(A,C));
  const c3 = imp(or(C, not(A)), B);
  return c1!=='F' && c2!=='F' && c3!=='F';
}
```

